IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Telecommunications (Emergency Call Service) Determination 2019 (ACMA, made under s 147 of the Act) -- what the
-- determination requires of the phone customer's provider when the customer calls 000, 112 or 106. Section 147(2)(a) of the Act
-- states free access only as an objective the ACMA must have regard to; the duties are in the determination, read here from the
-- deposited instrument. A module beside tcpss-customer.l4; it needs nothing from it and imports none of its names.
--
-- SEAT: the phone customer and end-user, as in tcpss-customer.l4. "Can I call Triple Zero free from my phone? Which number
-- works on which service? What must my provider pass on about my location? My provider says my mobile cannot reach Triple
-- Zero: what must it do, and by when? My emergency call failed during an outage: must anyone check on me?"
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 4, compilation date 1 November 2025, includes amendments F2025L00528 and F2025L01229 (register id
-- F2025C01030), deposited at ../../../telecommunications-emergency-call-service-determination-2019/registers/source-bundle/
-- F2019L01509.txt (retrieved 2026-10-23).
--
-- IN: ss 6 (emergency telephone service, exempt satellite service, emergency call, welfare check), 12-18, 23(2)-(4), 28,
-- 32, 33, 61, 62, 64-71. OUT: the carriers' and the emergency call persons' own duties (ss 8-11, 19-22, 24-27, 29-31A,
-- Part 3, Part 5, Part 6), which do not run to the customer; s 34-35 (charging emergency service organisations).
-- UNITS: s 69 counts "5 business days" for the first notice and calendar days (28 to 35) for ceasing supply; the rule below
-- keeps the two in separate obligations, each WITHIN its own count.

§ `Telecommunications (Emergency Call Service) Determination 2019 -- the caller's questions`

§§ `ss 12 to 18, 33 -- can I reach the emergency call service from my phone, free`

-- s 6: "emergency telephone service" is "(a) a standard telephone service; (b) a public mobile telecommunications service: (i)
-- that is not a standard telephone service; (ii) that enables the carriage of an emergency call; and (iii) for which the
-- customer equipment used to make the call does not provide a unique customer identifying number" (as deposited).
-- "exempt satellite service": a satellite service to a non-handheld satellite device, by an Inmarsat satellite, not under the
-- universal service obligation.
DECLARE `My service` IS ONE OF
    `a fixed phone line (a standard telephone service)`
    `a mobile phone service`
    `a satellite service, through a handset that can also use a mobile network`
    `a satellite service on another device, not exempt`
    `an exempt satellite service (a non-handheld Inmarsat device, not under the universal service obligation)`

DECLARE `Number I call` IS ONE OF
    `000`
    `112`
    `106 (the text emergency number)`

DECLARE `Can I reach the emergency call service` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my provider must give me access to the emergency call service for 000 and 112, free of charge (ss 15, 16, 33)`
    `yes: my provider must give me access to the 106 text emergency call service, free of charge (ss 17, 18, 33)`
    `no duty for this number on this service: use 000 (or 112 on a mobile)`
    `no: an exempt satellite service cannot be used for emergency calls, and my provider had to tell me so in writing before first supplying it (ss 12(2)(b), 13)`
    `not while a law requires or authorises my provider to stop it (for example a police request to suspend the service), or a matter beyond its control (a power failure at my premises, damaged wiring, my own call barring) prevents it (s 12)`

@export Must my phone provider give me access to Triple Zero (000), 112 or 106, free of charge (ss 12 to 18, 33)?
GIVEN `the service` IS A `My service` @desc What kind of service you are calling from: a fixed line, a mobile, a satellite service through a handset that can also use a mobile network, another satellite service, or an exempt (non-handheld Inmarsat) satellite service
      `the number` IS A `Number I call` @desc The emergency number you call
      `something stops access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law requires or authorises the provider to prevent access (such as a police request to suspend the service), or a matter beyond its control prevents it (a mains power failure at your premises, damaged wiring, a broken line, or call barring you set on your phone)
GIVETH A `Can I reach the emergency call service`
`Must my provider give me free access to emergency calls` MEANS
    IF `the service` EQUALS `an exempt satellite service (a non-handheld Inmarsat device, not under the universal service obligation)`
    THEN `no: an exempt satellite service cannot be used for emergency calls, and my provider had to tell me so in writing before first supplying it (ss 12(2)(b), 13)`
    ELSE IF `something stops access`
         THEN `not while a law requires or authorises my provider to stop it (for example a police request to suspend the service), or a matter beyond its control (a power failure at my premises, damaged wiring, my own call barring) prevents it (s 12)`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the number`
              -- s 15(1): 000 on an emergency telephone service; s 18: 106 on a satellite service capable of carrying it.
              WHEN `000` THEN
                  IF `the service` EQUALS `a satellite service on another device, not exempt`
                  THEN `no duty for this number on this service: use 000 (or 112 on a mobile)`
                  ELSE `yes: my provider must give me access to the emergency call service for 000 and 112, free of charge (ss 15, 16, 33)`
              -- s 15(2): 112 "on a public mobile telecommunications service"; s 16: 112 on a satellite service whose equipment
              -- "is capable of being used to access a public mobile telecommunications service".
              WHEN `112` THEN
                  IF `the service` EQUALS `a mobile phone service` OR `the service` EQUALS `a satellite service, through a handset that can also use a mobile network`
                  THEN `yes: my provider must give me access to the emergency call service for 000 and 112, free of charge (ss 15, 16, 33)`
                  ELSE `no duty for this number on this service: use 000 (or 112 on a mobile)`
              -- s 17: 106 on "a standard telephone service as described in subparagraph 6(1)(b)(ii) of the Act" (text);
              -- s 18: 106 on a satellite service "technically capable of carrying a call using that emergency service number".
              WHEN `106 (the text emergency number)` THEN
                  IF `the service` EQUALS `a mobile phone service`
                  THEN `no duty for this number on this service: use 000 (or 112 on a mobile)`
                  ELSE `yes: my provider must give me access to the 106 text emergency call service, free of charge (ss 17, 18, 33)`

-- s 14: access "whether or not a number is currently issued to the end-user in relation to a service".
@export Can I call Triple Zero from a service that has no phone number issued to me (s 14)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I call emergency services without a number issued to me` IF TRUE

§§ `s 23 -- what my provider must pass on with my emergency call`

DECLARE `What goes with my call` IS ONE OF
    `my phone number, as far as practicable, and the most precise location information available when I call (s 23(2), (4))`
    `the most precise location information available when I call; my number need not be sent because my mobile provider cannot identify it (s 23(3), (4))`

@export When I call 000, 112 or 106, what must my provider automatically send with the call (s 23)?
GIVEN `it is a mobile call to 000 or 112` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are calling 000 or 112 from a mobile service
      `my provider cannot identify my number` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider is unable to identify the number you are calling from (for example a phone with no SIM)
GIVETH A `What goes with my call`
`What must go with my emergency call` MEANS
    IF `it is a mobile call to 000 or 112` AND `my provider cannot identify my number`
    THEN `the most precise location information available when I call; my number need not be sent because my mobile provider cannot identify it (s 23(3), (4))`
    ELSE `my phone number, as far as practicable, and the most precise location information available when I call (s 23(2), (4))`

§§ `s 28 -- if my emergency call failed during a major outage`

-- s 28(1): "as soon as practicable after" becoming aware of (or being notified of) a major outage, the provider must
-- "undertake, or arrange to be undertaken, a welfare check on an end-user who made an unsuccessful emergency call during the
-- outage". (2): not if it cannot identify the call, is satisfied a later emergency call succeeded, or (mobile) the phone's
-- location has changed since. s 6 "welfare check": a phone call, then the police (fixed); two SMSs, then the police (mobile).
@export My emergency call did not get through during a major outage. Must my provider check on my welfare (s 28)?
GIVEN `it was during a major outage of my provider's network` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the call failed during a major outage affecting a network or facility your provider owns or operates (or one it was notified of)
      `my provider can identify the failed call` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider can identify that you made the unsuccessful emergency call
      `I later got through` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider is satisfied you later made a successful emergency call
      `I called from a mobile that has since moved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you called from a mobile and the provider has identified that the phone's location has changed since the call
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my provider do a welfare check` IF
        `it was during a major outage of my provider's network`
    AND `my provider can identify the failed call`
    AND (NOT `I later got through`)
    AND (NOT `I called from a mobile that has since moved`)

§§ `ss 61 to 71 -- if my mobile cannot reach the emergency call service`

-- s 64, 65: for a non-current customer (including a prospective one) whose phone is "not configured to be able to access the
-- emergency call service using both" the provider's network and, if it is unavailable, other networks, the provider "must:
-- (a) notify the end-user ...; (b) not supply carriage services to the mobile phone; and (c) provide the end-user with
-- information about alternative mobile phones ..., including ... low cost or no cost mobile phones". s 68, 69: for a phone that,
-- on or after 2 November 2024, can no longer access it, "within 5 business days, notify the end-user ... that the carriage
-- service provider will cease supply ... on a date that is between 28 and 35 days from the date of the notification"; send
-- "a further two separate notifications" before ceasing; cease "no earlier than 28 days" and "no later than 35 days" after;
-- and give information about alternative phones. s 70: its payment assistance policy must offer financial hardship customers
-- at least one way to get a low cost or no cost compatible phone. s 71: ss 65, 67, 69 do not apply to "a foreign traveller in
-- Australia who intends to remain in Australia for a period of no longer than 90 consecutive days" if the provider has sent the
-- phone a notification that it cannot reach the emergency call service.
DECLARE `My incompatible phone` IS ONE OF
    `my provider must tell me, must not supply service to this phone, and must tell me about other phones, including low cost or no cost ones (ss 64, 65)`
    `my provider must tell me within 5 business days that it will cut off service to this phone 28 to 35 days later, send two more notices first, tell me about other phones including low cost or no cost ones, and offer help through its payment assistance policy if I am in financial hardship (ss 68 to 70)`
    `as a short-stay foreign traveller I need only be sent a notice that the phone cannot reach the emergency call service; supply need not stop (s 71)`
    `nothing: my phone can reach the emergency call service on my provider's network and, if it is down, on other networks`

@export My mobile phone cannot reach Triple Zero on every network. What must my provider do (ss 64 to 71)?
GIVEN `my phone can reach it on all networks` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your phone is configured to reach the emergency call service on your provider's network and, if that is unavailable, on other public mobile networks
      `I am already its customer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are already a customer of the provider using this phone (rather than signing up or connecting for the first time)
      `I am a foreign traveller staying 90 days or less` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a foreign traveller in Australia intending to stay no longer than 90 consecutive days, and the provider has sent your phone a notice that it cannot reach the emergency call service
GIVETH A `My incompatible phone`
`What must my provider do about my incompatible phone` MEANS
    IF `my phone can reach it on all networks`
    THEN `nothing: my phone can reach the emergency call service on my provider's network and, if it is down, on other networks`
    ELSE IF `I am a foreign traveller staying 90 days or less`
         THEN `as a short-stay foreign traveller I need only be sent a notice that the phone cannot reach the emergency call service; supply need not stop (s 71)`
         ELSE IF `I am already its customer`
              THEN `my provider must tell me within 5 business days that it will cut off service to this phone 28 to 35 days later, send two more notices first, tell me about other phones including low cost or no cost ones, and offer help through its payment assistance policy if I am in financial hardship (ss 68 to 70)`
              ELSE `my provider must tell me, must not supply service to this phone, and must tell me about other phones, including low cost or no cost ones (ss 64, 65)`

GIVEN `the day of the first notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the earliest day service may stop` MEANS `the day of the first notice` PLUS 28

GIVEN `the day of the first notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the latest day service must stop` MEANS `the day of the first notice` PLUS 35

@export My provider has told me my mobile can no longer reach Triple Zero. Between which days must it stop service to the phone (s 69(4))?
GIVEN `the day of the first notice` IS A DATE @desc The date of the provider's first notification under s 69(2)
GIVETH A DATE
`The earliest day my provider may stop service to my phone` MEANS `the earliest day service may stop` `the day of the first notice`

@export My provider has told me my mobile can no longer reach Triple Zero. By what day must it have stopped service to the phone (s 69(4))?
GIVEN `the day of the first notice` IS A DATE @desc The date of the provider's first notification under s 69(2)
GIVETH A DATE
`The latest day my provider must stop service to my phone` MEANS `the latest day service must stop` `the day of the first notice`

§§ `The provider's duties, as rules`

DECLARE `ECS party` IS ONE OF
    `my provider`

DECLARE `ECS act` IS ONE OF
    `notify me that my phone can no longer reach the emergency call service`
    `send me a further notification`
    `cease supply to my phone`
    `give me access to the emergency call service`
    `charge me for the emergency call`
    `undertake a welfare check`

-- s 69(2): the first notice within 5 business days of identifying the phone (counted in business days); then (s 69(4)) cease
-- no earlier than 28 and no later than 35 days after that notice (counted in days from it). Ceasing before day 28 is itself a
-- breach, so the cease-supply step is a prohibition for 28 days followed by an obligation within the next 7.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ECS party` `ECS act`
`the s 69 duties` MEANS
    PARTY `my provider`
    MUST  `notify me that my phone can no longer reach the emergency call service`
    WITHIN 5
    HENCE
        PARTY `my provider`
        SHANT `cease supply to my phone`
        WITHIN 28
        HENCE
            PARTY `my provider`
            MUST  `cease supply to my phone`
            WITHIN 7
            LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 69(4)(b): supply not ceased within 35 days of the first notice"
        LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 69(4)(a): supply ceased earlier than 28 days after the first notice"
    LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 69(2): no notification within 5 business days"

-- s 69(3): "before ceasing supply ..., send a further two separate notifications". The rule has no number of days; it is
-- counted only as two notices in order.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ECS party` `ECS act`
`the s 69(3) further notices` MEANS
    PARTY `my provider`
    MUST  `send me a further notification`
    HENCE
        PARTY `my provider`
        MUST  `send me a further notification`
        LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 69(3): a second further notification must be sent before supply ceases"
    LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 69(3): two further notifications must be sent before supply ceases"

-- s 33: "A carriage service provider must give each end-user of the service access to emergency call services free of charge."
GIVETH A DEONTIC `ECS party` `ECS act`
`the s 33 free access duty` MEANS
    PARTY `my provider`
    SHANT `charge me for the emergency call`
    LEST  BREACH BY `my provider` BECAUSE "s 33: access to emergency call services must be free of charge"
